The window bundles SolidJS (MIT), three.js (MIT, https://threejs.org) for the 3D view, and the IBM
Plex fonts (SIL Open Font Licence 1.1). The desktop shell uses Tauri (MIT or Apache-2.0) and the
Rust crates listed in Cargo.lock, each under MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD, ISC, Zlib, MPL-2.0 or Unicode
licences (checked by cargo deny, deny.toml). The sparse Cholesky solver is faer (MIT,
https://github.com/sarah-quinones/faer-rs).

The unit table (crates/core/src/units.rs) is copied from Datum Calc (MIT, same author), with
factors from NIST SP 811 (2008). Densities and room-temperature properties of the materials come
from Datum Atlas (MIT, https://github.com/Edgar-Mendonca/Datum-Atlas), with each value's source;
the trends with temperature are typical values for each family from general engineering
handbooks, and the allowable stresses are typical values worked out from typical strengths (the
user checks them against their code edition).

Pipe outside diameters and wall thicknesses are the published nominal dimensions of wrought steel
pipe (ASME B36.10M) and stainless steel pipe (ASME B36.19M). Code checks are calculated using the
published equations of the process piping code (ASME B31.3); no tables, figures or text of any
code or standard are reproduced, and Datum Pipe is not affiliated with or endorsed by ASME. Where
each equation is published in the open literature is listed in Help › Sources (ui/src/help.tsx).
ASME and B31.3 are trademarks of the American Society of Mechanical Engineers.

The family workflow, ribbon, File page, updater and printing are taken from Datum Plan, by way of
Datum Mesh (both MIT, same author).
